associated with statistical consulting projects. In doing so, we seek to provide information that can benefit both statistical consultants and clients
as they consider how to develop their working relationships and agreements.
The research literature on statistical consulting is still developing. The existing materials focus considerably on applications and training statis
ticians in the collaborative skills of consulting (e.g., Bristol et al., 2020; Cabrera & McDougall, 2002). By contrast, relatively little information about
the contracting process in statistical consulting has been published. These contracts can greatly influence the resources available to the consultant
as the investigation is developed. The terms of the contract can also impact the working relationship between the client and the consultant. A
2006 survey of members of the Statistical Consulting Section of the American Statistical Association found that statistical consultants who
responded to the survey spent anywhere from zero to 2 % of their time on financial aspects of statistical consulting, which encompasses contracts
(Gullion & Berman, 2006). While this is not a considerable amount of time spent on contracts themselves, contracts are the basis of work done
for independent consultants who are self-employed. In fact, the survey found that far more self-employed consultants worked on their contracts
for services than did those working for contract-based, salaried consulting firms (Gullion & Berman, 2006). Meanwhile, clients may also lack
knowledge about statistical consulting services and the capabilities of a specific consultant (Svensson, 2000). To that end, there is a need for guid
ance on statistical contract types and selecting appropriate working agreements for the circumstances of a consulting engagement.
The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section 2 discusses several common financial structures for consulting contracts.
Section 3 compares a number of approaches to select the duration of statistical consulting contracts. Section 4 provides a discussion of the gen
eral risks of consulting contracts for clients and consultants. Section 5 enumerates the specialized risks associated with statistical investigations
and their associated risks for statistical consulting contracts. Section 6 concludes the paper with a discussion about the choices that clients and
consultants can make to create more mutually beneficial working arrangements.
2 | FINANCIAL CONSIDERATIONS OF CONSULTING CONTRACTS
Fundamentally, a statistical consultant offers labor and expertise to a client in exchange for a defined benefit. This may be financial in nature, such
as an honorarium or hourly fee. Benefits of other forms (e.g., co-authorship on a research publication, referrals to other potential clients, or equity
in a corporation) may be offered by the client as a substitute for or supplement to a financial fee (Cabrera & McDougall, 2002). Here we will focus
on wages as the primary contribution of a client to a consultant. The contract may specify that the fees will be paid to the consultant in a variety
of forms:
1. Project-based contracting: A defined and pre-determined benefit will be provided to the consultant in exchange for completion of the project's
aims.
2. Time-based contracting: The consultant receives a negotiated benefit for each unit of time (e.g., hour or day) dedicated to their work on the
project.
3. Retainer contracting: The consultant is paid a defined benefit for each period of time (e.g., per week or month), regardless of how much work
is performed on the project.
