risks. If the consultant underestimates the amount of work required to complete the project, then the effective hourly fee could be significantly
reduced (Ittenbach & DeAngelis, 2012). The consultant would be responsible for the burden of additional labor without financial remuneration.
Ethical issues may arise if either party can gain an unreasonable advantage, in wages or labor, from these terms. For both clients and consultants,
a clear understanding of the scope of work and agreement on the terms is critical to the success of project-based contracting (Peterson
et al., 2022).
Time-based billing has alternative risks. A consulting project may considerably exceed or fall well below the estimated number of hours dis
cussed in the contracting phase. In these circumstances, the client may take on far greater costs than anticipated, or the consultant may not earn
as much as expected. Time-based billing can incentivize a consultant to spend more time on a project, especially when alternative projects are not
readily available (Clark et al., 2019). This may not be the most efficient way to complete the statistical investigation. Billing for additional hours
may also harm the working relationship if the client becomes concerned about the associated expenses (Clark et al., 2019). Ethical issues may also
arise when the financial incentives for additional billing do not serve the client's interest in terms of the additional work that is developed. Time
based billing also creates asymmetric incentives in terms of the responsibility for generating opportunities for billable work. If there is little or no
work to be done for a period of time, the consultant loses the opportunity to earn money, while the client only incurs costs when the need for sta
tistical consulting arises.
Retainer fees provide certainty in costs and wages for a period of time. In this respect, consulting contracts based on retainer fees are more
similar to a salaried job (part-time or full-time) than to project-based or time-based consulting contracts. Relative to time-based billing, a client
who pays a retainer fee has a greater incentive to make requests and create projects for a consultant (Clark et al., 2019). Otherwise, the consul
tant will be paid without the requirement to provide support. However, the client's requests may greatly exceed the expectations of the
consultant in terms of the time required. Within a retainer agreement, an increasing volume of demands from the client without additional finan
cial reward may create a burden for the consultant (Clark et al., 2019). Meanwhile, the client may be less aware of the adverse consequences that
excessive requests may create. Many of these risks invert the corresponding concerns of project-based consulting agreements.
Many variations of these basic fee structures are possible. Project-based billing may provide contingencies for additional payments if a maxi
mum number of hours is exceeded. Time-based billing may include a defined minimum or maximum number of hours. Contracts based on retainer
fees might include clauses to ensure the consultant's availability for a minimum amount of time during the contracting period. Many contracts
may use combinations of these forms, such as paying a retainer fee for a defined period of time plus an hourly rate when additional work is
needed. Some clients or consultants may view the agreed terms as strict requirements, with norms of careful accounting for the work performed
and the time utilized. Other working relationships may be more flexible so long as both parties agree on the billing practices.
3 | THE DURATION OF CONSULTING CONTRACTS
Consulting contracts can also define the length of time allotted for the collaboration. Some possibilities include:
1. Project-based duration: The contract lasts as long as is required to complete the project.
2. Time-based duration: The contract defines a fixed time period in which the consulting project may be performed.
3. Evergreen contracts: The contract creates an ongoing working relationship between the client and the consultant.
