4 | GENERAL RISKS OF STATISTICAL CONSULTING CONTRACTS
Statistical consulting shares a number of common elements with other forms of consulting. Collaborations between clients and statistical consul
tants have a number of risks that are more general to consulting work. Some of these issues may include (A) unanticipated developments in the
project or working relationship, (B) issues related to the degree of revision and collaboration required to complete the project in a satisfactory
manner, and (C) issues pertaining to changing scopes as a consulting project develops. For each of these categories, we provide examples of gen
eral consulting risks below:
Category A: Unanticipated developments
1. The project encounters delays due to factors outside of at least one party's control. For instance, the client's organization could face turnover,
with departures from some of the project's contributors.
2. Materials or information provided by the client do not match the requirements of the project or may be unreliable. This may lead to delays and
additional work to clarify the issues at different stages of the project.
3. The consultant may have difficulty completing some of the project's goals in a timely fashion.
4. A missed or late payment from a client may delay further developments from the consultant.
Category B: Revisions and collaboration
5. The client's goals for the project were not clearly articulated at the time of the agreement.
6. Revisions are required to address mistakes in the methods or analyses or to clarify misunderstandings about the investigation between the cli
ent and the consultant (Svensson, 2000).
7. New questions within the project's scope arise based upon a review of the preliminary exploration or results.
8. Portions of the work (e.g., writing a report) require more rounds of revision than what was anticipated.
9. Discussions may be expanded to include a wider range of participants within the client's organization (Tolulope, 2022).
10. The client and consultant may have different expectations about the manner in which the project's results are presented or the type of mate
rials that will be produced.
Category C: Changing scopes of projects
11. The client requests additional investigations or outputs that were not part of the project's original scope.
12. The consultant offers to perform additional work that was not part of the project's original scope.
13. There may be disagreements between the client and the consultant about whether aspects of the investigation fit within the project's original
scope.
