Many of these concerns cannot be fully anticipated by either the consultant or the client before a project begins. A statistical consultant may
not be able to review the investigation's data until a contract has been negotiated. Unexpected issues are common in messy data sets and can
require significant time to address. Iterative development that incorporates feedback from the client can improve the quality of the investigation,
but it also introduces variability into the amount of labor and time required to complete the project. The nature of statistical collaborations may
also vary considerably. A statistical consultant may not adequately estimate the effort that will be required to guide the client. In practice, a statis
tical consultant may face multiple issues that each expand upon the requirements of developing a successful investigation.
6 | DISCUSSION
The risk of a statistical consulting project includes both the general risks of consulting agreements along with the specialized risks of statistical
investigations. Contracts are designed to create opportunities while mitigating the potential risks (Zahn & Isenberg, 1983). A relatively simple con
tract may be the most straightforward way to proceed from preliminary discussions to beginning the project, but it may not necessarily serve the
interests of either the consultant or the client. Some degree of negotiation in the contracting process can help each party reach a stronger agree
ment. At the same time, a tense negotiation can create its own obstacles to an effective collaboration and the ultimate success of the project.
Statistical consultants have the opportunity to develop individual approaches to collaborating with clients depending on the type and length
of the contract. For example, time-based contracting might be suited for more short-term, transactional working relationships. Project-based bill
ing may be helpful for short-term or medium-term needs (Cabrera & McDougall, 2002). Longer-term relationships might be better served by
retainer-based contracts (Shippey, 2003).
No single style of contract will address the needs of every consultant, client, or project. However, this analysis of the risks and benefits of dif
fering forms of contracts can provide some situational guidance for statistical consultants and clients. Consultants who seek one-time, shorter
term projects with clients might consider a project-based fee if they believe they can complete the work quickly. Proposing an hourly rate can be
helpful for projects with less certainty in the duration. Retainer fees are beneficial for consultants who would like to work with a client for a longer
period of time, as they allow for some of the certainty in fees of a traditional job.
Negotiating a minimum number of hours is helpful for time-based contracts, while setting a maximum would be helpful with project-based
billing and retainer models. A consultant might also consider charging a premium for clients with urgent requests (Bristol et al., 2020) or a history
of delays in projects. By contrast, clients that offer regular, ongoing work might provide sufficient volume so that a consultant could charge a
lower hourly rate while still earning a greater volume over time based on increased utilization. Evergreen contracts reduce the barriers to taking
on additional projects, but project-based or time-based durations may help consultants or clients to ensure a definitive end to the project to pur
sue other commitments. Combining evergreen contracts with a retainer agreement may be more appropriate for longer-term collaborations, espe
cially among parties with more developed and mature working relationships. Likewise, working agreements that balance risks and rewards may
also be suitable for longer term collaborations and partnerships.
Meanwhile, statistical consultants should consider the inherent risks of statistical analyses as they negotiate contracts. Anticipating the poten
tial for delays in projects, allotting significant time for data cleaning, and budgeting for additional rounds of revision can create more realistic esti
mates of the time required for projects. This may also require raising awareness of these potential risks with clients in the contracting process.
Clients may be wary of an increase in the estimated hours or fees for a project. However, consultants can mitigate these risks in several ways.
The consulting contract may be split into phases (Tolulope, 2022), allowing for a revised estimate of the hours after some exploration of the data
is complete. A consultant could also offer a discount on an hourly fee in exchange for a higher guaranteed number of hours of work, creating
mutual benefits for both parties.
Any contract is necessarily an approximation of the working relationship between separate entities. What is codified may not encompass the
full scope of the collaboration. Consulting relationships with a client may also evolve considerably over the course of one or many projects. Work
ing norms may informally capture the residual differences between the contractual relationship and the true nature of the collaboration. This can
also create risks for consultants, as the expectations of what can be produced in a project or for a defined period of time might increase without
commensurate changes in the fees. Offering favors on one project may create unreasonable expectations for ongoing work. A consultant may
have to work additional unpaid hours to complete a project.
By understanding the risks and rewards of different models of contracting, a statistical consultant may be able to create improved conditions
for a working relationship with a client. Negotiating the terms of a contract can lead to collaborations that are less adversarial and more mutually
beneficial. This will empower statisticians and clients to produce a higher quality of work. It may also create more opportunities for lasting working
